I'm joined today by Daniel Willingham  who is a psychologist at the University  of Virginia where he is a professor in  the Department of Psychology his  research focuses on the applications of  findings from cognitive psychology and  neuroscience to K through 12 education  it's so great to speak to you I recently  read a very interesting article which  discussed the idea of whether listening  to an audio book rather than reading it  in the traditional sense is in any way  cheating and I think to have this  conversation we first sort of have to  decide what would count as cheating so  what what do people generally mean when  they say is it cheating it's a great  question because I feel like when you  say listening to an audio book is in  some sense cheating very few people say  what what are you talking about what  does that even mean so everyone has some  intuitive sense of what's meant by that  but it's not quite as obvious when you  unpack it what what I take it to mean  what I take cheating to mean is cheating  is when you get some sort of a resource  you get some sort of a goodie even  though you haven't done the work that is  supposed to be done in order to get  access to that goodie so it feels like  that what people are saying is listening  to an audiobook is in some way easier  and therefore you're getting the  pleasure of having reading the book or  also possibly the credit of having read  the book hmm without credit sort of  among your peers yet to say yes I read  that book without having put the work in  I believe that what you mentioned last  there the sort of credit in society is  really the aspect of it that that people  are mostly referring to because when I  think about it if you when we when we  talk about cheating the questions I  would have if you listen to an audio  book are how is your level of  comprehension affected by having  listened to it rather than having read  it we know about the very positive  effect on vocabulary that regular  reading has do you get the same benefit  from listening to the book or not so  let's start first there with with that  what does research tell us about  the benefits of reading a book in  written form versus listening to it so  if there is someone who is a very fluent  decoder is the is the lingo which means  that decoding is the process of looking  at print on the page and sort of  sounding it out in your mind getting  words from print that's on the page  that's decoding so if you're very fluent  at that you're very experienced in other  words if you're an adult a typical adult  reader there's enormous overlap between  reading something in print on the page  and listening to it they're not  identical processes but most of what  most of the language processing that  your mind does when you're reading is  really piggybacking on language  processes that are used in conversation  and just sort of normal oral language  okay so there are differences but lots  of overlap okay  so what do we know then about areas  where the sort of net effect on an  individual from listening listening to  an audio book versus reading it might  differ so and again a lot of this is  less about formal experiments that have  been done on this and more sort of  things that we know about reading so you  can sort of reason where things would be  different one way in which listening to  an audio book is going to be different  is that you get you the person who's  reading it provides what's called  prosody prosody is the sort of melody of  speech and prosody carries information  so for example if I said to you hey what  a great party this is that's one thing  whereas if I say oh what a great party  this is right the difference between  sarcasm and sincerity in that case is a  hundred percent based on prosody it's  the pacing of which words I emphasize  and so on now that's invisible on the  page right and prosody actually matters  to how we understand speech and and even  some sort of low-level processes like  figuring out which is what what's the  subject of the sentence what's the verb  and the sentence and so forth so that's  a notable difference now how frequently  does that make a real importance of  financial difference in comprehension  the fact that in listening you get  prosody in reading you have to provide  it yourself the answer to that is not  known it's probably not that often I  mean the extreme version that I can  imagine is something like reading a  Shakespearean play where for you know  Americans living today a Shakespearean  English is really a different dialect of  English and so it's a struggle for us to  piece it together so they're having that  extra cue would help a lot if you're  reading verses listening to the latest  Dan Brown thriller Pross he's probably  not going to make such a big difference  what do we know if anything and or what  might your speculation be depending on  what the answer is to the first part  when it comes to probably more  nonfiction than fiction what do we know  or could we expect about the long-term  retention of material in terms of  listening to a nonfiction book be it a  biography or a science book or whatever  versus reading it writes a great  question and I would say if  comprehension is the same then you would  expect the retention to be comparable so  I think here we get to the other  difference when you mention nonfiction I  think we get to another important  difference between listening to a book  and reading a book which is pacing when  I'm reading from print pacing is  completely under my own control whereas  when I'm listening sort of in principle  sure I guess I can go you know stop the  CD and go back and listen to something  again but I'm probably a whole lot less  likely to do that than I would be in  print and we know when people are  reading print eye movements actually a  substantial proportion of them something  like maybe 10 15 percent are actually  backward eye movements where you're  going back in to the beginning of a  sentence or an earlier part of a  sentence to reread something those you  don't even notice but of course you very  much feel it when you sort of get to the  bottom of page and you suddenly say gosh  I just realized I don't I don't really  understand what I've been reading let me  let me go back a little bit and try  again so this is where something like  listening to a textbook that's full of  new and for me  that's going to be pretty complex we  would expect that to be pretty difficult  and probably again this is less about  retention and more about online  comprehension that's going to be  compromised bottom line when we get back  to this idea of the concept of cheating  with audio books is there any way in  which we can say that there is any  advantage other than maybe that you  could listen to an audio book more  quickly than you would read it is there  anything else that we could call an  advantage of or or anything that could  even remotely approach what we would  call cheating by listening the only  advantage and I think this gets back to  what we were saying at that at the top  of our discussion the only way I think  you could call it an advantage is that  it feels like it's easier to multitask  and when you're listening you mentioned  that listening would be faster less what  is actually slower is that right I mean  it does make sense because your internal  dialogue may and you're sort of silent  reading voice may actually be faster  than the speed at which an audio book is  recorded absolutely for most people it  is and you can try this yourself again  if you're if you're fluent decoder and  you're a typical adult reader you will  read faster than you can then you can  speak so that's actually not an  advantage but it does feel like it's  easier to do something else I can listen  to an audio book in the car I can listen  to an audio book when I'm doing dishes  or something like that  and that may in principle I guess you  can multitask while you're reading a  print book too I mean you and I could be  having a conversation and I'm sort of  trying to read a print book at the same  time people tend not to do that right so  yeah probably is a difference in how  readily you can multitask and that may  be the the sense in which people feel  like it's cheating it's like gosh you  and I are in this book club and I read  the paper version and I had to carve out  time to make sure that I could get to it  and so on and now I find out you were  doing the audiobook shucks I could have  been in my car listening to the  audiobook and and I would have saved  myself some time so that may be  important in the cheating this idea of  how easily you can multitask well when I  think about it and get back to the end  I'm convinced that the entire cheating  aspect is more social stigma and the the  prototypical book club is exactly where  I would think about it where you come in  and you say oh you know I didn't get my  reading done this week on either to die  and then someone says oh I'm done with  the book because I've just been  listening to it on the way to and from  work that person or for better or worse  is going to be critique critiqued by  someone as having taken a shortcut  aren't they I think that's right and  again I have nothing to go on here but  anecdote but I'll tell you since I wrote  that piece about audiobooks and cheating  lots of people have emailed and called  me and many of them have brought up book  clubs in particular as the place that I  get attacked for having listened to a  book absolutely so there you go  absolutely fascinating Daniel Willingham  I believe that this will also become  more of an issue as we move forward in  terms of technology and and more  technological usage of how people learn  the idea of sped up recordings and the  idea of being able to condense  I know people who listen to podcasts and  they listen to them at 2x speed and they  say you know I'm finding I'm getting 90%  of what I would get but I'm listening in  half the time I believe this will have  more implications in the next five years  that's that's a really interesting  observation my sense is the same as  yours and let me just encourage all of  your listeners and viewers of this video  you should not only watch this in real  time but you should watch it several  times absolutely no doubt about it all  right professor of psychology Daniel  Willingham thank you so much for talking  to me today my pleasure
 

